The unfamiliar-reader review is written to `review-unfamiliar.json` in the run directory. It parses against `reviewSchema`, both hashes match the frozen draft and evidence, and every quoted passage is an exact substring of `draft.md`. I touched no other repo file and published nothing.

**Verdict from the cold-reader seat:** the profile works. A reader who knows nothing about streaming finishes knowing who she is, what she built, what it costs her, and why the same record supports both Type 2 and Type 3. The problems are orientation, not argument.

**One major finding**
- **December 2025 show paragraph:** it packs six items and three unintroduced names (Fanfan, Caseoh, Piker) into one block. A newcomer can't tell which jokes she told versus her cohost, and the "You're a pedophile!" quote has no gloss, so it can be read as a joke told on stage or an accusation.

**Seven minor findings**
- **Opening:** it doesn't say what the Streamer Awards are until paragraph four, and the article never says why Frost was shouting.
- **Wedding episode:** "the pattern ran small" names a pattern the reader can't identify, and it stalls the turn into the Type 3 case.
- **Atrioc:** he is introduced only as "the streamer", so the stakes of forgiving him and skipping the party are unclear.
- **Podcast and cast:** the body never says she co-hosts a podcast, which leaves "her cohost Valkyrae" ambiguous, and Ahgren's trophy win doesn't remind the reader he's her partner.
- **Jargon:** "swatted" is unexplained even though the Type 6 dismissal rests on it; "rerun channel", "parasocial", "clip farm" and Twitch are also unglossed.
- **Sh\*tcon vs Sh\*tcamp:** the two names read as the same event or a typo.
- **Money:** "the bill had tripled" has no baseline, and the million-dollar figure isn't attributed to the interviewer.

**Protected passages (five):** the "Both are things a host does" turn, the narrower-test sentence in the type section, the reading of the hotel quote, "The woman who knows which guest will be alone at the table says nobody knows her," and the closing paragraph.

**Similarity:** the advisory tripped on two units against the Olivia Dean draft — the standard not-a-diagnosis line and a one-clause Type 2 definition. I raised no finding; both are ordinary type vocabulary. I compared only the units quoted in the advisory and did not open the other draft.

**Risks:** carried forward unchanged (sensitive allegations, contested typing); no new risk raised. I did not reopen sources, since that belongs to the evidence role, so the acceptance tests that need a fact are conditional on what the opened sources support.
